      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the OIML certificate.\n\nKey information:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02, Revision 3\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Applicant: GWF AG, Obergrundstrasse 119, CH-6005 Luzern, Switzerland\n- Manufacturer: GWF AG, same address\n- Certified type: Water meter – ultrasonic, dry dial, sonico NANO\n- Module designation: \"-\" (dash, meaning not applicable)\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Page total: 10 pages\n- Date issued: 11 March 2026 (from signature date and revision 3 date)\n\nType evaluation report: 0511-ER-V124-25 dated 11 March 2026, 39 pages\nTest reports:\n- 6015-PT-P0022-25, 74 pages including annex 1\n- 6011-PT-SW011-25, 7 pages including annex 1\n- 6011-PT-SW019-24, 7 pages including annex 1\n- 6015-PT-P5006-24, 206 pages including annex 1-2\n- 6011-PT-SW008-24, 7 pages including annex 1-2\n- 6015-PT-P0003-26, 113 pages including annex 1-2\n- 6011-PT-SW033-25, 7 pages including annex 1-2\n\nDocumentation file: 0511-UL-V124-25\n\nRevision history:\n- \"-\" / 30 May 2024 / Issuing certificate\n- Revision 1 / 20 September 2024 / Added new SW\n- Revision 2 / 12 June 2025 / Added new sizes DN25 to DN40 and new SW and CRC checksum versions\n- Revision 3 / 11 March 2026 / Addition of ratio for water meter DN25 from R630 to R1000, new SW version and addition of module NB-IoT and adding mechanical environment class according to OIML R 49-1:2013: B, O\n\nIssuing authority signatory: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\n\nModel family: The cert mentions DN25 to DN40 sizes (DN25, DN32, DN40 possibly) — but the exact model list isn't given in this fragment. Revision 2 says \"Added new sizes DN25 to DN40\". The detailed characteristics are in additional pages (part of the full cert, not this fragment). Should I include model_family? The fragment mentions DN25 to DN40 but doesn't enumerate models. Hmm. I could note it, but rule 8 says don't fabricate. I might mention DN25–DN40 in description. I'll leave model_family out or include minimal info? The revision history mentions DN25 to DN40. Without explicit enumeration, I shouldn't fabricate individual models. I'll omit model_family but could mention in description.\n\nCharacteristics mentioned in description:\n- Volume resolution: 0.00001 m³ on digital display\n- Battery: 3.6V mains battery (powered by mains battery 3.6V)\n- Environmental classification: M (mechanical class... actually M with classes B, O per R 49-1)\n- Electromagnetic environmental class: E2\n\nShould I put these in characteristics.type_level? The description says \"displays the volume resolution of 0.00001 m³\". I could add:\n- volume_resolution: {value: 0.00001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n\nAccuracy class: 2 → recommendation.accuracy_classes: [2] (numeric or string? schema says accuracy_classes[], I'll use [\"2\"] or [2]... \"2\" is the class designator for water meters. I'll use \"2\" as string since class designators can be letters. Actually for R49 classes are numeric (1, 2). I'll use [\"2\"].\n\nRecommendation id: \"R49\" canonical. Edition: 2013 (int).\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- page_total: 10\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2026-03-11\" (the certificate revision 3 date; issuing date 30 May 2024 originally, but current issue date 11 March 2026). The date on signature: 11 March 2026. I'll use \"2026-03-11\".\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present... Actually the \"V 3112\" mark? That's probably a CMI mark. Not clear it's the issuer ID. Omit.\n- revision: \"3\" — hmm, the schema doesn't have a revision field at certificate level, but revision_history covers it. The certificate number includes Revision 3. I could include \"revision\": \"3\"? The schema doesn't list it but revision_history does. I'll stick to schema; maybe add revision in certificate as extra? Rule says follow schema; omit.\n\nIssuing authority:\n- name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"] — or one line? I'll split: \"Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\" could be one line or split. I'll split into lines: \"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\".\n- person_responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- person_title: \"Head of Certification Body\" — that's Pavel Klenovský's title though. The signature block: \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". The person responsible on page 1 is Jan Kalandra. So person_title may not belong to Jan Kalandra. Hmm. I'll include person_title: \"Head of Certification Body\" — but it applies to the signatory. Ambiguous. Maybe include both? Schema has person_responsible and person_title. The signer is a different person. I could add person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and leave title null, or note the signer separately. I'll include person_title as null... Actually, safer: person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". For the signature, I can note in... there's no signer field. I'll add person_title: \"Head of Certification Body\"? That would wrongly attribute to Kalandra. Hmm. Many extraction tasks would attribute the title to the signing person. But the schema only has one person field. I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\" and omit title, or... Alternatively include title since it's the title of the authority's certification body head. I'll include it cautiously? Risk of misattribution. I'll omit person_title to be safe. Actually, hmm — could include \"Head of Certification Body\" as it's the title within the issuing authority. I think it's acceptable but potentially misleading. I'll omit.\n\nActually, wait — maybe I should include the signatory info somewhere. The schema for issuing_authority has person_responsible. Page 1 says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". That's the clear mapping. The signature block is the signing authority head. I'll just use Jan Kalandra.\n\nWebsite/phone/email: not present. Omit.\noiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\nApplicants: GWF AG with address.\nManufacturers: GWF AG with address.\n\nCertified type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" (per R49). The category examples list doesn't include water meter but says e.g. — \"Water meter\" is fine.\n- type_designations: [\"sonico NANO\"]\n- module_designation: \"-\" means not applicable → \"Not applicable\"? The dash indicates none. I'll use null or \"Not applicable\". Schema allows \"Not applicable\" | null. Dash → \"Not applicable\". Hmm, actually safer: null. The doc explicitly has \"-\" which means not applicable. I'll use \"Not applicable\".\n- description: The measuring system description text. I'll provide a summary combining the description paragraphs.\n\nComponents: The description says water meters consist of cast brass body, ultrasonic transducers, electronic indicating device. Also NB-IoT communication module (not certified), impulse module (not part of certificate). Should I record components? The cert names sub-assemblies. NB-IoT module: not certified itself but mentioned. I could add components: measuring transducer (ultrasonic), indicating device. Hmm, role/type_designations. There aren't type designations for components. I might skip components or include minimal entries. Rule 6 says for complex instruments with named components. The sonico NANO is described as one device with internal parts. I think it's borderline; I'll include light components: ultrasonic transducers (measuring transducer), electronic indicating device (calculating/indicating device), NB-IoT module (communication, not certified). Actually I'll include them with roles and no type designations... \"Don't fabricate\" — roles are descriptive. I think including them adds value. But risk of noise. The description is clear: \"consist of a cast brass body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and the electronic indicating device\". I'll include components:\n- role: \"measuring transducer\", type_designations: [] — hmm, \"one pair of ultrasonic transducers\". \n- role: \"indicating device\" (electronic indicating device with LCD display)\n- role: \"communication module (not certified)\" NB-IoT.\n\nI'll include these with a note in role or characteristics. Keep simple.\n\nTest reports:\n- id \"0511-ER-V124-25\", date 2026-03-11, pages 39, role \"type_evaluation_report\"\n- 6015-PT-P0022-25, pages 74, role test_report\n- 6011-PT-SW011-25, 7 pages\n- 6011-PT-SW019-24, 7\n- 6015-PT-P5006-24, 206\n- 6011-PT-SW008-24, 7\n- 6015-PT-P0003-26, 113\n- 6011-PT-SW033-25, 7\nDates for test reports: not given individually. Omit dates.\nPages: 74 \"including annex 1\" — pages: 74.\n\nDocumentation file: 0511-UL-V124-25 → test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\", id 0511-UL-V124-25. No date/pages.\n\nRevision history:\n- revision \"0\"? The first row has \"-\" for revision no, date 30 May 2024, \"Issuing certificate\". The schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". I'll use \"-\" as given? Example says e.g. \"0\" not 0 — meaning string. The doc uses \"-\". I'll use \"-\" to be faithful. Hmm, could map to \"0\". I'll keep \"-\" as it appears... Actually the rule example shows revision \"0\" — suggesting initial issuance might be represented as \"0\". But the doc literally says \"-\". I'll use \"-\" since it's what's present. Hmm, both defensible. I'll use \"-\".\n\n- \"Revision 1\", 20 September 2024, \"Added new SW\"\n- \"Revision 2\", 12 June 2025, \"Added new sizes DN25 to DN40 and new SW and CRC checksum versions\"\n- \"Revision 3\", 11 March 2026, \"Addition of ratio for water meter DN25 from R630 to R1000, new SW version and addition of module NB-IoT and adding mechanical environment class according to OIML R 49-1:2013: B, O\"\n\nFootnotes: none.\n\nCharacteristics type_level:\n- volume_resolution: {value: 0.00001, unit_symbol: \"m³\", unit_id: null} — from description. Actually text: \"displays the volume resolution of 0.00001 m³ on the digital display\".\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V — \"powered mains battery 3.6V\". I'll name it battery_voltage.\n- indication_update_interval: every two minutes → value 2, unit \"min\"? \"displays the indication of each volume on the display every two minutes\". Could record: update_interval: 2 min. Maybe include.\n\nMarking info: environmental class M, E2, mechanical environment class B, O (from revision 3). These are classification attributes. I could add:\n- mechanical_environment_class: \"B, O\" (per R 49-1:2013)\n- electromagnetic_environment_class: \"E2\"\n\nThese appear in marking list and revision. I'll include electromagnetic_environment_class E2 and mechanical environment classes B and O. Hmm, value as string \"B, O\"? Could do value: \"B, O\". Fine.\n\nActually careful: the marking section lists \"Environmental classification (M)\" — that's the standard R49 marking M (M1?). The mechanical class B, O mentioned in revision 3. I'll include mechanical_environment_classes: \"B, O\".\n\nShould I be conservative? The instructions want structured extraction. I'll include a reasonable set.\n\nDate format: The schema examples use dates like \"11 March 2026\" in markdown. I'll output ISO \"2026-03-11\"? Not specified. Safer to output ISO 8601. Many extractors keep as-is. I'll use ISO format for dates.\n\ncertificate.date_issued: 11 March 2026 (date of signature and latest revision). Original issue 30 May 2024. I'll use \"2026-03-11\".\n\nproject_number: not present. Omit.\n\nNow model_family: fragment mentions DN25 to DN40 sizes and DN25 ratio R630 to R1000. Not enumerating models. I'll omit model_family (detailed characteristics in additional pages not in this fragment). Maybe mention in description that sizes DN25–DN40 covered. I'll add to description.\n\nOutput JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 10,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2026-03-11\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"GWF AG\", \"address_lines\": [\"Obergrundstrasse 119\", \"CH-6005 Luzern\", \"Switzerland\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"GWF AG\", \"address_lines\": [\"Obergrundstrasse 119\", \"CH-6005 Luzern\", \"Switzerland\"]}\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"sonico NANO\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"Not applicable\",\n    \"description\": \"Ultrasonic water meter with dry dial and electronic indicating device (LCD display showing volume and flow). Cast brass body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and electronic indicating device. Volume resolution 0.00001 m³ on the digital display. No buttons; legally non-relevant communication possible via NFC sensor on the register. Software separation between legally relevant and non-relevant parts; CRC and SW version of the legally relevant part shown in the auto-rounding menu on the LCD display. Displays separately delivered volume for reverse and forward flow every two minutes; permanently shown volume is the difference. Optional impulse module (not part of this certificate) and internal NB-IoT communication module (not certified itself, no influence on metrological parameters). Powered by 3.6 V battery. Sizes DN25 to DN40 (added at Revision 2); ratio for DN25 extended from R630 to R1000 at Revision 3. Can be installed to operate in any positions.\"\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"measuring transducer\", \"type_designations\": [], \"characteristics\": {\"description\": \"one pair of ultrasonic transducers\"}},\n    ...\n  ]\n\nHmm — components schema: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. For transducers type_designations empty. Maybe simpler to include only NB-IoT and impulse module as noted components? Actually the \"components\" section is meant for named sub-assemblies. I'll include:\n- {\"role\": \"measuring transducer (ultrasonic)\", \"type_designations\": []}\n- {\"role\": \"electronic indicating device\", \"type_designations\": []}\n- {\"role\": \"communication module\", \"type_designations\": [\"NB-IoT\"], \"characteristics\": {\"certification_status\": {\"value\": \"not certified itself; no influence on metrological parameters\"}}}\n\nHmm, characteristics values must be structured objects {value, unit_symbol, unit_id}. Per rule 2, \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object\". But components' characteristics{} is generic dict. I'll keep it minimal or use structured form. For NB-IoT: characteristics: {\"certified\": {\"value\": false, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}}? That seems overkill. Maybe skip characteristics for components. For impulse module: not part of certificate — skip entirely or include with note. I'll include NB-IoT since it's part of the meter as of revision 3, and impulse module with a note. Actually \"can be equipped by impulse module which is not part of this certificate\" — optional accessory not certified. I'll skip impulse module to reduce noise, or include. I'll include both briefly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: components list may cause clutter. The cert is a simple water meter — no component type designations. Rule 6 targets complex instruments with designated modules (like FM01, P2, WB). Here, there are none. I'll omit components entirely. The NB-IoT module detail is captured in description. Hmm, but NB-IoT is a named module. I'll include a small components array with NB-IoT only? I think including the NB-IoT module is useful since it was added at revision 3. I'll include:\n\n\"components\": [\n  {\"role\": \"communication module (internal, not certified itself; no influence on metrological parameters)\", \"type_designations\": [\"NB-IoT\"]}\n]\n\nHmm, role should be short: \"communication module\". Add note in characteristics: {\"certification_status\": {\"value\": \"not certified itself; no influence on metrological parameters\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}}. OK.\n\ncharacteristics:\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"volume_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.00001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"indication_update_interval\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"min\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"mechanical_environment_class\": {\"value\": \"B, O\", ...},\n  \"electromagnetic_environment_class\": {\"value\": \"E2\", ...}\n}\n\nHmm, mechanical class B, O: from revision 3 \"adding mechanical environment class according to OIML R 49-1:2013: B, O\". Marking section says \"Environmental classification (M)\". I'll include mechanical_environment_classes with value \"B, O\" per R 49-1:2013. And electromagnetic_environment_class \"E2\" from markings. Acceptable.\n\nAlso \"accuracy_class 2\" is at recommendation level.\n\nrecommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2013, \"scheme\": \"A\", \"accuracy_classes\": [\"2\"]}\n\nHmm accuracy class \"2\" — could be number 2 or string \"2\". For R49, classes are numeric (1, 2) but as designators. I'll use [\"2\"] string? The example R60 uses accuracy_classes[] e.g. [\"C\", \"D\"]. For water meters, class \"2\". I'll use [\"2\"].\n\ntest_reports: as listed. The type evaluation report role \"type_evaluation_report\", test reports \"test_report\", documentation file \"documentation_file\".\n\n{\n  \"id\": \"0511-ER-V124-25\",\n  \"date\": \"2026-03-11\",\n  \"pages\": 39,\n  \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"\n}\n\nTest reports pages: \"74 pages including annex 1\" → pages: 74. \"206 pages including annex 1 – 2\" → 206.\n\nrevision_history: as above.\n\nShould certificate include \"revision\": \"3\"? Schema doesn't include. But revision_history covers. I'll skip.\n\ndate_issued: Which date? The original issuing 30 May 2024, latest revision date 11 March 2026 (signature date). I'll use \"2026-03-11\" as date_issued (current). Reasonable.\n\nPage total: \"Page 1 of 10 pages\" → 10.\n\nFormat of dates: I'll use ISO. Fine.\n\nFinal JSON — ensure no trailing commentary, pure JSON.",
